{"abstract":"Antibiotic resistance, driven by misuse and overuse of antibiotics, is one of the greatest threats against human health. The antimicrobial pressure during prolonged antibiotic treatment of chronic bacterial infections selects for resistance. While antibiotic combinations may reduce resistance emergence, antibiotic-tolerant persister cells can serve as a reservoir for resistance development. Therefore, targeting these cells with anti-persister drugs might provide a novel strategy for resistance prevention. In this study, we conducted 42 days of adaptive laboratory evolution using Staphylococcus aureus exposed to rifampicin, ciprofloxacin, daptomycin, and vancomycin, alone or in combination with the anti-persister drug mitomycin C. We monitored antibiotic susceptibility daily and assessed phenotypic changes in growth and biofilm formation in evolved strains. Whole-genome sequencing revealed mutations linked to antibiotic resistance and phenotypic shifts. Rifampicin resistance developed within a few days, while ciprofloxacin and daptomycin emerged in approximately 3 weeks. Treatments with vancomycin or mitomycin C resulted in minimal changes in susceptibility. While combination therapy delayed resistance, it did not fully prevent it. Notably, the combination of rifampicin with mitomycin C maintained rifampicin susceptibility throughout the long-term evolution experiment. Sub-inhibitory antibiotic treatments selected for both previously characterized and novel mutations, including unprecedented alterations in the nucleotide excision repair system and azoreductase following mitomycin C exposure. The delayed resistance development observed with combination therapy, particularly mitomycin C’s ability to suppress rifampicin resistance, suggests potential therapeutic applications. Future studies should evaluate the clinical efficacy of anti-persister drugs in preventing resistance across different bacterial pathogens and infection models.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Petersen, Maiken Engelbrecht","Khamas, Amanda Batoul","Østergaard, Lars Jørgen","Jørgensen, Nis Pedersen","Meyer, Rikke Louise"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-806475"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Meyer, Rikke Louise"],"keyword":["AMR","Adaptive mutations","Antibiotic resistance","Staphylococcus aureus"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"combination therapy delays antimicrobial resistance after adaptive laboratory evolution of staphylococcus aureus","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1128/aac.01483-24","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-806475","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11963546","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"105001974308","type":"scopus"},{"id":"40084881","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-54c90ca4-8b9f-4b74-88da-ead151566566"],"keyword":["AMR","Staphylococcus aureus","adaptive mutations","antibiotic resistance"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-8997B08C7C29349F1543FD11CAF26FA1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1128/aac.01483-24","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Maiken Engelbrecht","key":"petersenpau","last":"Petersen","locid":"99c8dbe5-12c1-4b7b-bc1d-a2985885e525","name":"Petersen, Maiken Engelbrecht","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Amanda Batoul","key":"khamaspau","last":"Khamas","locid":"2543fe5d-ede4-44e0-8a06-17ad5ce8fc37","name":"Khamas, Amanda Batoul","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lars Jørgen","key":"østergaardpau","last":"Østergaard","locid":"fb8358a6-187a-4d5f-8187-06051ed74173","name":"Østergaard, Lars Jørgen","orcid":"0000-0003-2459-0511","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Nis Pedersen","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"ac385492-f707-4053-bc6e-965ba8f60360","name":"Jørgensen, Nis Pedersen","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rikke Louise","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","locid":"f1caa4f6-bc6e-459a-a976-8843c7ea7ff3","name":"Meyer, Rikke Louise","orcid":"0000-0002-6485-5134","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40084881"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1128/aac.01483-24","issn":["00664804","10706283"],"issn_org":["00664804"],"issue":"4","journal":"Antimicrobial Agents and Chemotherapy","paperid":"e01483-24","publisher":"American Society for Microbiology","status":"p","title":"Antimicrobial Agents and Chemotherapy","volume":"69"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105001974308"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-54c90ca4-8b9f-4b74-88da-ead151566566"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Combination therapy delays antimicrobial resistance after adaptive laboratory evolution of Staphylococcus aureus","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-806475","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Implant-associated osteomyelitis (IAOM) is a severe complication of the usage of orthopaedic implants. IAOM is difficult to treat and infection relapse is often due to insufficient treatment of bacterial persister cells. The anthelmintic drug bithionol is promising in combating persister cells - including Staphylococcus aureus persister cells. We investigated bithionol alone and in combination with antibiotics both in vitro and in vivo, using a murine IAOM model. In vitro experiments confirmed bithionol's anti-persister activity against S. aureus, demonstrating significant CFU reductions in combination with daptomycin and moxifloxacin. In the murine IAOM model, moxifloxacin led to a small but significant reduction in bacterial load in bone of approx. log 0.5 CFU/ml. However, combining bithionol with antibiotics did not further reduce bacterial load in either bone or on implants. We subsequently demonstrated dramatically reduced activity of bithionol in the presence of albumin, suggesting low bioavailability in vivo. Despite promising in vitro results, bithionol's efficacy against persister cells did not translate into improving treatment outcome the IAOM model. The study highlights the challenges in translating in vitro findings to in vivo outcomes, and future research into application of bithionol to treat bacterial infections must first address its bioavailability or investigate local delivery options.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Seefeldt, Anders Marthinsen","Johansen, Mikkel Illemann","Sillesen, Freja Winther","Petersen, Maiken Engelbrecht","Østergaard, Lars Jørgen","Meyer, Rikke Louise","Jørgensen, Nis Pedersen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-815512"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Animals","Anti Bacterial Agents pharmacology","Disease Models Animal","Female","Mice","Microbial Sensitivity Tests","Osteomyelitis drug therapy","Prostheses and Implants adverse effects","Prosthesis Related Infections drug therapy","Staphylococcal Infections drug therapy","Staphylococcus aureus drug effects"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"bithionol is ineffective in a mouse model of s aureus implant associated osteomyelitis despite potent in vitro activity","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-08879-2","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-815512","identifier":[{"id":"PMC12230103","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"105010113003","type":"scopus"},{"id":"40619546","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-378d7b21-7f70-4b3f-9100-c501f9b48b49"],"keyword":["Animals","Anti-Bacterial Agents/pharmacology","Bacterial Load/drug effects","Daptomycin/pharmacology","Disease Models, Animal","Female","Mice","Microbial Sensitivity Tests","Moxifloxacin/pharmacology","Osteomyelitis/drug therapy","Prostheses and Implants/adverse effects","Prosthesis-Related Infections/drug therapy","Staphylococcal Infections/drug therapy","Staphylococcus aureus/drug effects"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-F28BB083ABB4B37402415AD4A740C4C8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-08879-2","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Infectious Diseases"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders Marthinsen","key":"seefeldtpau","last":"Seefeldt","name":"Seefeldt, Anders Marthinsen","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mikkel Illemann","key":"johansenpau","last":"Johansen","locid":"521aaf8b-4d7a-4672-82c4-2c79a297b02a","name":"Johansen, Mikkel Illemann","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Freja Winther","key":"sillesenpau","last":"Sillesen","locid":"b83fc643-da0b-4b4e-9ba9-1641e0bcfca2","name":"Sillesen, Freja Winther","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Maiken Engelbrecht","key":"petersenpau","last":"Petersen","locid":"99c8dbe5-12c1-4b7b-bc1d-a2985885e525","name":"Petersen, Maiken Engelbrecht","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lars","key":"østergaardpau","last":"Østergaard","locid":"fb8358a6-187a-4d5f-8187-06051ed74173","name":"Østergaard, Lars Jørgen","name_org":"Østergaard, Lars","orcid":"0000-0003-2459-0511","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rikke Louise","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","locid":"f1caa4f6-bc6e-459a-a976-8843c7ea7ff3","name":"Meyer, Rikke Louise","orcid":"0000-0002-6485-5134","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Nis Pedersen","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"ac385492-f707-4053-bc6e-965ba8f60360","name":"Jørgensen, Nis Pedersen","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40619546"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41598-025-08879-2","issn":["20452322"],"issue":"1","journal":"Scientific Reports","paperid":"24156","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Scientific Reports","volume":"15"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105010113003"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-378d7b21-7f70-4b3f-9100-c501f9b48b49"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Bithionol is ineffective in a mouse model of S. aureus implant-associated osteomyelitis despite potent in vitro activity","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-815512","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Novel approaches for the prevention of biofilm-mediated oral diseases aim to control dental biofilms rather than eradicating bacteria in the mouth. One such approach is the use of enzymes that specifically target and degrade the dental biofilm matrix and thereby facilitate biofilm removal. Matrix-degrading enzymes have consistently shown promising results in vitro, but data on in situ-grown oral biofilms are limited. This study aimed to investigate the effect of combined treatment with mutanase, beta-glucanase and DNase on in situ biofilm formation and removal, microbial biofilm composition and biofilm pH. Biofilms from healthy participants were grown for 48 or 72 h on lower-jaw splints and enzyme or control-treated during (3x/day, 30 min) or after growth (30 min). Under the tested conditions, enzyme treatment had no significant effect on biofilm formation or removal compared to control, as assessed by optical coherence tomography and confocal microscopy. Likewise, enzymatic treatment did not induce significant changes in the microbial composition of the biofilms that were dominated by Streptococcus, Haemophilus, Neisseria, Veillonella and Fusobacterium species. The biofilm pH response to a sucrose challenge was assessed using confocal microscopy-based pH ratiometry, and the average biofilm pH was not significantly different between the intervention groups. Under the conditions employed in this study, the tested enzymes had no significant impact on in situ grown biofilms. The treatment regimen, the biofilm composition, or the analytical methods employed may explain the difference to previous results. Further studies are warranted to assess the therapeutic potential of multi-enzyme treatment for dental biofilm control.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Rikvold, Pernille","Møllebjerg, Andreas","Raittio, Eero","Nielsen, Signe Maria","Johnsen, Karina Kambourakis","Lund, Marie Braad","Jørgensen, Mette Rose","Meyer, Rikke Louise","Schlafer, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-816161"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Rikvold, Pernille","Schlafer, Sebastian"],"country":["Finland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Eastern Finland"],"keyword":["Confocal microscopy","Dental plaque","Optical coherence tomography","Sequence Analysis RNA"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-NOSI"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-NOSI","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the effect of multiple enzyme treatment on in situ oral biofilm formation in healthy participants","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bioflm.2025.100298","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-816161","identifier":[{"id":"105009006048","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-8b309cee-5347-4c04-b601-a74d3f119f7b"],"keyword":["Confocal Microscopy","Dental plaque","Enzyme Therapy","Optical coherence tomography","Sequence Analysis, RNA"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-7035D08F1A7B975AE8D69BCD4B125CBB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bioflm.2025.100298","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Dentistry and Oral Health, Department of Dentistry and Oral Health - Section for Oral Ecology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"novonesis","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR