,"l3":"DI-PR-NOSI","l3nn":"Novonesis","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Novonesis","namevar":["Novozymes A/S"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-NOSI","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Andreas","key":"møllebjergpau","last":"Møllebjerg","locid":"4176d27b-9e84-42e2-9357-cd93796c3046","name":"Møllebjerg, Andreas","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Lorena Gonzales","key":"palménpau","last":"Palmén","name":"Palmén, Lorena Gonzales","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Klaus","key":"goripau","last":"Gori","name":"Gori, Klaus","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rikke Louise","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","locid":"f1caa4f6-bc6e-459a-a976-8843c7ea7ff3","name":"Meyer, Rikke Louise","orcid":"0000-0002-6485-5134","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35980226"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1128/spectrum.02880-22","issn":["21650497"],"issue":"5","journal":"Microbiology Spectrum","paperid":"e0288022","publisher":"American Society for Microbiology","status":"p","title":"Microbiology Spectrum","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85140856562"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Private Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-42418e5b-06e7-4086-bc05-ca60601d6452"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Erratum for Møllebjerg et al., \"The Bacterial Life Cycle in Textiles Is Governed by Fiber Hydrophobicity\"","type":"Journal Comment","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-167062","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Staphylococcus saccharolyticus, a coagulase-negative staphylococcal species, has some unusual characteristics for human-associated staphylococci, such as slow growth and its preference for anoxic culture conditions. This species is a relatively abundant member of the human skin microbiota, but its microbiological properties, as well as the pathogenic potential, have scarcely been investigated so far, despite being occasionally isolated from different types of infections including orthopedic implant-associated infections. Here, we investigated the growth and biofilm properties of clinical isolates of S. saccharolyticus and determined host cell responses. Growth assessments in anoxic and oxic conditions revealed strain-dependent outcomes, as some strains can also grow aerobically. All tested strains of S. saccharolyticus were able to form biofilm in a microtiter plate assay. Strain-dependent differences were determined by optical coherence tomography, revealing that medium supplementation with glucose and sodium chloride enhanced biofilm formation. Visualization of the biofilm by confocal laser scanning microscopy revealed the role of extracellular DNA in the biofilm structure. In addition to attached biofilms, S. saccharolyticus also formed bacterial aggregates at an early stage of growth. Transcriptome analysis of biofilm-grown versus planktonic cells revealed a set of upregulated genes in biofilm-embedded cells, including factors involved in adhesion, colonization, and competition such as epidermin, type I toxin-antitoxin system, and phenol-soluble modulins (beta and epsilon). To investigate consequences for the host after encountering S. saccharolyticus, cytokine profiling and host cell viability were assessed by infection experiments with differentiated THP-1 cells. The microorganism strongly triggered the secretion of the tested pro-inflammatory cyto- and chemokines IL-6, IL-8, and TNF-alpha, determined at 24 h post-infection. S. saccharolyticus was less cytotoxic than Staphylococcus aureus. Taken together, the results indicate that S. saccharolyticus has substantial pathogenic potential. Thus, it can be a potential cause of orthopedic implant-associated infections and other types of deep-seated infections.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Afshar, Mastaneh","Møllebjerg, Andreas","Minero, Gabriel Antonio Salvador","Hollensteiner, Jacqueline","Poehlein, Anja","Himmelbach, Axel","Lange, Jeppe","Meyer, Rikke Louise","Brüggemann, Holger"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-201420"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Afshar, Mastaneh","Brüggemann, Holger"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Leibnitz Institute of Plant Genetics and Crop Plant Research (IPK)","University of Göttingen"],"keyword":["Anaerobes","Biofilm","Coagulase negative staphylococci","Implant associated infection","Inflammation","Staphylococcus saccharolyticus","Transcriptome"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"biofilm formation and inflammatory potential of i staphylococcus saccharolyticus i a possible cause of orthopedic implant associated infections","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2022.1070201","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-201420","identifier":[{"id":"36519178","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85143986792","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC9742538","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-7dfcdc91-10b3-4428-bac4-02f6a3e12c89"],"keyword":["Staphylococcus saccharolyticus","anaerobes","biofilm","coagulase-negative staphylococci","implant-associated infection","inflammation","transcriptome"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6AEFCA17B0DBE2CDC2A35CA051D8254B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2022.1070201","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Biomedicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"university of göttingen","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Göttingen","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"leibnitz institute of plant genetics and crop plant research","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Leibnitz Institute of Plant Genetics and Crop Plant Research (IPK)","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mastaneh","key":"afsharpau","last":"Afshar","locid":"2977cdc1-1114-441c-84f7-ae6e31738ff7","name":"Afshar, Mastaneh","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Andreas","key":"møllebjergpau","last":"Møllebjerg","locid":"4176d27b-9e84-42e2-9357-cd93796c3046","name":"Møllebjerg, Andreas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Gabriel Antonio","key":"mineropau","last":"Minero","locid":"cc436bce-1546-4216-bb08-ae06ac0300d2","name":"Minero, Gabriel Antonio Salvador","name_org":"Minero, Gabriel Antonio","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jacqueline","key":"hollensteinerpau","last":"Hollensteiner","name":"Hollensteiner, Jacqueline","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anja","key":"poehleinpau","last":"Poehlein","name":"Poehlein, Anja","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Axel","key":"himmelbachpau","last":"Himmelbach","name":"Himmelbach, Axel","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jeppe","key":"langepau","last":"Lange","locid":"a2d13696-fb79-4a18-8951-3839a84f1038","name":"Lange, Jeppe","orcid":"0000-0001-6868-7513","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rikke Louise","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","locid":"f1caa4f6-bc6e-459a-a976-8843c7ea7ff3","name":"Meyer, Rikke Louise","orcid":"0000-0002-6485-5134","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"brüggemannpau","last":"Brüggemann","locid":"029d7735-40a7-4a8f-a552-67e496015144","name":"Brüggemann, Holger","orcid":"0000-0001-7433-0190","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36519178"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/fmicb.2022.1070201","issn":["1664302X"],"journal":"Frontiers in Microbiology","paperid":"1070201","publisher":"Frontiers Media SA","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Microbiology","volume":"13"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85143986792"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-7dfcdc91-10b3-4428-bac4-02f6a3e12c89"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Biofilm formation and inflammatory potential of <i>Staphylococcus saccharolyticus</i> : A possible cause of orthopedic implant-associated infections","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-201420","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Staphylococcus aureus is a widespread and highly virulent pathogen that can cause superficial and invasive infections. Interactions between S. aureus surface receptors and the extracellular matrix protein fibronectin mediate the bacterial invasion of host cells and is implicated in the colonization of medical implant surfaces. In this study, we investigate the role of distribution of both fibronectin and cellular receptors on the adhesion of S. aureus to interfaces as a model for primary adhesion at tissue interfaces or biomaterials. We present fibronectin in patches of systematically varied size (100-1000 nm) in a background of protein and bacteria rejecting chemistry based on PLL-g-PEG and studied S. aureus adhesion under flow. We developed a single molecule imaging assay for localizing fibronectin binding receptors on the surface of S. aureus via the super-resolution DNA points accumulation for imaging in nanoscale topography (DNA-PAINT) technique. Our results indicate that S. aureus adhesion to fibronectin biointerfaces is regulated by the size of available ligand patterns, with an adhesion threshold of 300 nm and larger. DNA-PAINT was used to visualize fibronectin binding receptor organization in situ at ∼7 nm localization precision and with a surface density of 38-46 μm-2, revealing that the engagement of two or more receptors is required for strong S. aureus adhesion to fibronectin biointerfaces.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Khateb, Heba","Sørensen, Rasmus Schøler","Cramer, Kimberly","Eklund, Alexandra S.","Kjems, Jørgen","Meyer, Rikke Louise","Jungmann, Ralf","Sutherland, Duncan S"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-265651"],"filters":{"added":"2022-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Sutherland, Duncan S"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Ludwig Maximilian University","Max Planck Institute of Biochemistry"],"keyword":["Colloidal lithography","DNA paint","Fibronectin","protein nanopattern"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the role of nanoscale distribution of fibronectin in the adhesion of staphylococcus aureus studied by protein patterning and dna paint","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acsnano.2c00630","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-265651","identifier":[{"id":"PMC9330902","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85136014263","type":"scopus"},{"id":"35801826","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-eed6fe74-a3be-4778-becf-6dc23a6b5c8b"],"keyword":["DNA-PAINT","Staphylococcus aureus adhesion","colloidal lithography","fibronectin","fibronectin binding protein localization","protein nanopattern"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-CBAF4A345ABE23BFF22E45B61590B301"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acsnano.2c00630","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9330902/pdf/nn2c00630.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"max planck institute of biochemistry","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Max Planck Institute of Biochemistry","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"ludwig maximilian university","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Ludwig Maximilian University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Heba","key":"khatebpau","last":"Khateb","locid":"d98c6ee8-14ca-4dd9-afbb-71f6f71bed40","name":"Khateb, Heba","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus S.","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"dd13ff30-6bd7-4512-bc60-efa5c5b0ebdd","name":"Sørensen, Rasmus Schøler","name_org":"Sørensen, Rasmus S.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kimberly","key":"cramerpau","last":"Cramer","name":"Cramer, Kimberly","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Alexandra S.","key":"eklundpau","last":"Eklund","name":"Eklund, Alexandra S.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jorgen","key":"kjemspau","last":"Kjems","locid":"f5915d75-aca3-437a-9a9d-f4fdfef7accd","name":"Kjems, Jørgen","name_org":"Kjems, Jorgen","orcid":"0000-0003-4128-9317","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rikke L.","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","locid":"f1caa4f6-bc6e-459a-a976-8843c7ea7ff3","name":"Meyer, Rikke Louise","name_org":"Meyer, Rikke L.","orcid":"0000-0002-6485-5134","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Ralf","key":"jungmannpau","last":"Jungmann","name":"Jungmann, Ralf","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Duncan S.","key":"sutherlandpau","last":"Sutherland","locid":"5c5c563f-ca05-4219-b60d-8a3d6303ff2f","name":"Sutherland, Duncan S","name_org":"Sutherland, Duncan S.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35801826"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1021/acsnano.2c00630","issn":["19360851","1936086X"],"issn_org":["19360851"],"issue":"7","journal":"ACS Nano","pages":"10392-10403","publisher":"American Chemical Society","status":"p","title":"ACS Nano","volume":"16"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85136014263"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-eed6fe74-a3be-4778-becf-6dc23a6b5c8b"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"The Role of Nanoscale Distribution of Fibronectin in the Adhesion of Staphylococcus aureus Studied by Protein Patterning and DNA-PAINT","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-265651","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Biofouling is one of the biggest impediments to the production of clean and safe water by membrane filtration. As contaminated water is filtered through the membrane, organic matter and microorganisms accumulate on the surface to form a biofilm which obstructs the water flow. A range of strategies have been developed to tackle this issue. Microorganisms and their nutrients can be removed from the feed water by different pretreatment methods to limit colonization and proliferation of microorganisms on the membrane. Furthermore, the membrane surface can be modified to achieve anti-adhesive, biocidal, or quorum quenching properties. These fouling prevention strategies can only delay the development of a biofilm, and the membranes must eventually be cleaned. Physical cleaning methods include air bubbles or ultrasound, but the most commonly used methods use chemical cleaning by applying biocides, acids, caustics, chelators, and surfactants. Chemical cleaning agents come with environmental and economic drawbacks, and biological cleaning solutions are therefore under development. These solutions include treatment with bacteriophages, quorum sensing inhibitors, and matrix-degrading enzymes. Despite extensive research on combatting biofouling, there is still no anti-fouling panacea. However, the additive effect of feedwater pretreatment, anti-adhesive membrane surfaces and the use of gentle biological membrane cleaning methods is bringing the industry closer to long-lasting mitigation of biofouling.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Møllebjerg, Andreas","Meyer, Rikke Louise"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-169568"],"filters":{"added":"2022-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-NOSI"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-NOSI","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"biofouling control in water filtration systems","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-10992-8_20","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-169568","ids":["au-468bc437-8e0e-4902-a34c-545392da571a"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-9DBD7F0FACDBE22F32DE08752EEA1D78"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center - INANO-MBG, iNANO-huset"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"novonesis","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-NOSI","l3nn":"Novonesis","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Novonesis","namevar":["Novozymes A/S"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-NOSI","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Andreas","key":"møllebjergpau","last":"Møllebjerg","locid":"4176d27b-9e84-42e2-9357-cd93796c3046","name":"Møllebjerg, Andreas","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rikke Louise","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","locid":"f1caa4f6-bc6e-459a-a976-8843c7ea7ff3","name":"Meyer, Rikke Louise","orcid":"0000-0002-6485-5134","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-10992-8_20","eisbn":["9783031109928"],"isbn":["9783031109911"],"pages":"521-551","place":"Cham","publisher":"Springer","series":"Springer Series on Biofilms","status":"p","subtitle":"Current and Future Applications to Prevent, Control and Eradicate Biofilms","title":"Antibiofilm Strategies"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Private Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-468bc437-8e0e-4902-a34c-545392da571a"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Biofouling Control in Water Filtration Systems","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-169568","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Staphylococcus epidermidis causes some of the most hard-to-treat clinical infections by forming biofilms: Multicellular communities of bacteria encased in a protective matrix, supporting immune evasion and tolerance against antibiotics. Biofilms occur most commonly on medical implants, and a key event in implant colonization is the robust adherence to the surface, facilitated by interactions between bacterial surface proteins and host matrix components. S. epidermidis is equipped with a giant adhesive protein, Embp, which facilitates bacterial interactions with surface-deposited, but not soluble fibronectin. The structural basis behind this selective binding process has remained obscure. Using a suite of single-cell and single-molecule analysis techniques, we show that S. epidermidis is capable of such distinction because Embp binds specifically to fibrillated fibronectin on surfaces, while ignoring globular fibronectin in solution. S. epidermidis adherence is critically dependent on multi-valent interactions involving 50 fibronectin-binding repeats of Embp. This unusual, Velcro-like interaction proved critical for colonization of surfaces under high flow, making this newly identified attachment mechanism particularly relevant for colonization of intravascular devices, such as prosthetic heart valves or vascular grafts. Other biofilm-forming pathogens, such as Staphylococcus aureus, express homologs of Embp and likely deploy the same mechanism for surface colonization. Our results may open for a novel direction in efforts to combat devastating, biofilm-associated infections, as the development of implant materials that steer the conformation of adsorbed proteins is a much more manageable task than avoiding protein adsorption altogether.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Khan, Nasar","Aslan, Hüsnü","Büttner, Henning","Rohde, Holger","Golbek, Thaddeus W.","Roeters, Steven J.","Woutersen, Sander","Weidner, Tobias","Meyer, Rikke Louise"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-272515"],"filters":{"added":"2022-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Meyer, Rikke Louise"],"country":["Germany","Netherlands"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Amsterdam","University of Hamburg"],"keyword":["AFM","AUREUS","Adhesin","Atomic force microscopy","Bacterial Adhesion","Binding","Chemistry","Epidermidis","Fibronectin","Identification","Infections","Other","Plasma","Staphylococcus epidermidis"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the giant staphylococcal protein embp facilitates colonization of surfaces through velcro like attachment to fibrillated fibronectin","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.7554/elife.76164","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-272515","identifier":[{"id":"000830554800001","type":"wos"},{"id":"35796649","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC9302970","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85133881361","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-fb012b0b-fe1f-4a9c-b0c0-a898c4ffd6f9"],"keyword":["ADHESIN","AFM","ATOMIC-FORCE MICROSCOPY","AUREUS","BINDING","CHEMISTRY","EPIDERMIDIS","Embp","IDENTIFICATION","INFECTIONS","Other","PLASMA","SDRG","Staphylococcus epidermidis","bacterial adhesion","fibronectin","multivalent"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-CF36CF49C1468D2F79B2BB8C24E3FD2E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.7554/elife.76164","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center - INANO-Bioscience, iNANO-huset"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"university of hamburg","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Hamburg","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"nl","key":"university of amsterdam","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Amsterdam","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nasar","key":"khanpau","last":"Khan","locid":"33b169a1-1e78-4233-849a-f3b8284d8331","name":"Khan, Nasar","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hüsnü","key":"aslanpau","last":"Aslan","locid":"05e8885e-db36-4f96-8ecf-5263c578def8","name":"Aslan, Hüsnü","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Henning","key":"büttnerpau","last":"Büttner","name":"Büttner, Henning","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"rohdepau","last":"Rohde","name":"Rohde, Holger","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thaddeus Wayne","key":"golbekpau","last":"Golbek","locid":"0fa79311-6553-451b-b607-7a5408d66af6","name":"Golbek, Thaddeus W.","name_org":"Golbek, Thaddeus Wayne","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Steven Joop","key":"roeterspau","last":"Roeters","locid":"c76b17db-0e9b-4988-b0e9-81012389655b","name":"Roeters, Steven J.","name_org":"Roeters, Steven Joop","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Sander","key":"woutersenpau","last":"Woutersen","name":"Woutersen, Sander","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Tobias","key":"weidnerpau","last":"Weidner","locid":"7a001432-fe35-4c21-b32c-40a65e060b03","name":"Weidner, Tobias","orcid":"0000-0002-7083-7004","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rikke Louise","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","locid":"f1caa4f6-bc6e-459a-a976-8843c7ea7ff3","name":"Meyer, Rikke Louise","orcid":"0000-0002-6485-5134","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35796649"],"pub":{"doi":"10.7554/elife.76164","issn":["2050084X"],"journal":"eLife","paperid":"e76164","publisher":"eLife Sciences Publications Ltd","status":"p","title":"eLife","volume":"11"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85133881361"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-fb012b0b-fe1f-4a9c-b0c0-a898c4ffd6f9"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"The giant staphylococcal protein Embp facilitates colonization of surfaces through Velcro-like attachment to fibrillated fibronectin","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-272515","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Introduction: The COVID-19 pandemic impacted profoundly on the wellbeing and social interactions of the world population, and all dimensions of sexual health were potentially affected by globally implemented preventive measures. Objectives: The scoping review aimed to compile existing research investigating possible effects of COVID-19 lockdowns on adult sexual health, that is, sexual behavior, functioning, and satisfaction. Further, studies on the interplay between mental health and sexual well-being during the pandemic were reviewed. Methods: The review was conducted in accordance with guidelines established by the Joanna Briggs Institute and the Extension for Scoping Reviews (PRISMA-ScR) Checklist. On October 11–12, 2021, PubMed, Embase, PsycInfo, Cinahl, Cochrane, Sociological Abstracts and Scopus were systematically searched for relevant peer-reviewed papers employing quantitative methodology. Additionally, unpublished (“grey”) research studies on the subject were retrieved. The screening, data extraction, and analysis of evidence were conducted by 4 independent reviewers using an iterative approach. Results: Based on 107 studies included, the scoping review showed that the pandemic had had a wide impact on all dimensions of sexual health. Except for solo sex activities, mainly negative COVID-19 implications were identified, although findings were, in sum, characterized by complexity and unpredictability. Thus, sexual behavior, functioning, and satisfaction during the pandemic appeared to be mitigated by a broad range of sociodemographic and contextual factors. Finally, sexual health seemed deeply entwined with overall mental health. Conclusion: The scoping review revealed a broad range of COVID-19-related effects on sexual health, including an overall decline in partnered sex and a concurrent increase in solo sex activities. It also emphasized a need for future research to shed light on possible long-term consequences of the pandemic in various population groups and on all aspects of sexual health. Toldam NE, Graugaard C, Meyer R, et al. Sexual Health During COVID-19: A Scoping Review. Sex Med Rev 2022;10:714–753.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Toldam, Nana Ernst","Graugaard, Christian","Meyer, Rikke","Thomsen, Louise","Dreier, Sabine","Jannini, Emmanuele A.","Giraldi, Annamaria"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-000152"],"filters":{"added":"2022-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Giraldi, Annamaria"],"country":["Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Rome Tor Vergata"],"keyword":["Covid 19","Sexual Behavior","Sexual dysfunction","Sexual function","Sexual health"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sexual health during covid 000000000019 a scoping review","src":["aau","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/about/copyright/","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9242892/pdf/main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-000152","identifier":[{"id":"85136630052","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC9242892","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"36030177","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-02f5e9c3-74b8-4e47-b268-9f36712d8f52","ku-22dddb38-c6c9-429d-97e1-af1080cc9ce6"],"keyword":["COVID-19","Sexual Behavior","Sexual Dysfunction","Sexual Function","Sexual Health"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-93F9BA1227666FE56A150662BC93342B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/about/copyright/","type":"rem","url":"https://ww