{"abstract":"This paper presents a course planning model for lower and upper secondary schools in the fields of digital literacy and computational thinking. The examples in the paper are based on a Danish regional project entitled \"crossingIT,\" in which about 35 unique courses were developed and conducted by local educators. Duration of the courses ranged from approximately two hours to four a week for fifteen weeks. The model highlights four perspectives in course planning: (1) a traditional planning perspective, with a focus on learning objectives, learning activities, practical organisation, evaluations, etc; (2) methods for teaching digital production such as iterative design cycles, pair programming pedagogy and video tutorials as well as textbooks; (3) the field of digital literacy including, for example, computational thinking skills, ethics, critical thinking and societal perspectives; and (4) Environment, including local company participation, career learning and cross-school teaching. The model can be used for both planning and analysing courses in the fields of digital literacy and computational thinking. The article offers specific examples of teaching methods and specific cases from practice. Teaching in this field does not have a long tradition in Denmark. The model provides specific advice for well-rounded didactic planning in the fields of computational thinking and digital literacy.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Majgaard, Gunver","Lamscheck-Nielsen, Regina"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-575263"],"event":{"acronym":"ECEL","end":"2019-11-08","place":"Copenhagen, dk","start":"2019-11-07","title":"18th European Conference on e-Learning (ECEL19)","year":"2019"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Majgaard, Gunver"],"keyword":["Coding and programming","Computational thinking","Digital literacy","Learning","Teaching"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"digital literacy and course design","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/173753941/Majgaard_19_ECEL.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-575263","identifier":[{"id":"85077502497","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-0740587f-1c66-4bb0-bd69-e85c848dff57"],"keyword":["Coding and programming","Computational thinking","Course design and didactics","Digital literacy","Learning","Teaching"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-F425581C5845ADAD56F4476195C60C2F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/173753941/Majgaard_19_ECEL.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute","SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute, SDU Biorobotics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"other companies","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-OTH","l3nl":"Other Companies","l3nn":"Other Companies","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Other Companies","namevar":["Moeve aps"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-OTH","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Gunver","key":"majgaardpau","last":"Majgaard","locid":"fab5735b-60ae-443c-95b5-fea8fd899b41","name":"Majgaard, Gunver","orcid":"0000-0001-5203-3224","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Regina","key":"lamschecknielsenpau","last":"Lamscheck-Nielsen","name":"Lamscheck-Nielsen, Regina","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Rikke","key":"orngreenpedh","last":"Orngreen","name":"Orngreen, Rikke","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Mie","key":"buhlpedh","last":"Buhl","name":"Buhl, Mie","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Bente","key":"meyerpedh","last":"Meyer","name":"Meyer, Bente","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.34190/eel.19.115","eisbn":["9781912764419"],"isbn":["9781912764426"],"pages":"369-377","publisher":"Academic Conferences and Publishing International","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 18th European Conference on e-Learning ECEL 2019"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85077502497"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Other Companies","Private Research","Universities","University of Southern Denmark","moeve aps"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-0740587f-1c66-4bb0-bd69-e85c848dff57"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Digital Literacy and Course Design","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-575263","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"For about ten years gamification has been a buzzword in business, and for about 20 years a topic of research in academia. Despite much commercial interest in and a potentially huge market for successful products, for instance, in the areas of education and health, much excitement is still based on speculation, reception in parts of the academic community remains sceptical, and a pervasive application in many areas of everyday life is arguably yet to happen. It might be time to take stock: By collating observations from multiple empirical studies and meta-studies, this survey identifies, briefly presents and discusses definitions, aims, applications, strategies and specifically challenges of gamification. While gamification is far from being the first or the only notion to describe attempts to connect play with purposes beyond itself, this study is based on research that identifies itself as being focused on gamification. This study does not aim to exhaustively list experiences or results of gamification, or to carry out a systematic review of the field, but to collect and highlight issues that need to be resolved or mitigated for gamification to progress. It finds problematic definitions, unclear strategies, a low number of empirical studies, methodological problems, mixed and partial results, non-uniform user behaviours, a predominant focus of studies on low-level behavioural effects and short-term effects, as well as undesirable side-effects of gamification.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Cermak-Sassenrath, Daniel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-677323"],"filters":{"added":"2023-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Empirical studies","Gamification","Methodological Problems","Short term Effects","Speculation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"current challenges in gamification identified in empirical studies","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.34190/EEL.19.065","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-677323","identifier":[{"id":"85077510850","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-8e4e0c8f-ab74-4098-b27b-9a332381e110"],"keyword":["Empirical Studies","Gamification","Methodological Problems","Short-term Effects","Speculation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-C79696A503572F7FFFA06E310F09AB3D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.34190/EEL.19.065","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.academic-conferences.org/wp-content/uploads/dlm_uploads/2019/11/ECEL-Abstract-Booklet-Embedded.pdf","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86378121/current_challenges_in_gamification_published_version.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Media, Art & Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel","key":"cermaksassenrathpau","last":"Cermak-Sassenrath","name":"Cermak-Sassenrath, Daniel","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Rikke","key":"ørngreenpedh","last":"Ørngreen","name":"Ørngreen, Rikke","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Mie","key":"buhlpedh","last":"Buhl","name":"Buhl, Mie","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Bente","key":"meyerpedh","last":"Meyer","name":"Meyer, Bente","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.34190/eel.19.065","eisbn":["9781510899025"],"pages":"119-27","place":"Reading","publisher":"Academic Conferences and Publishing International","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 18th European Conference on e-Learning (ECEL) 2019"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85077510850"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8e4e0c8f-ab74-4098-b27b-9a332381e110"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Current challenges in gamification identified in empirical studies","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-677323","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Objective: To investigate whether a whole grain diet alters the gut microbiome and insulin sensitivity, as well as biomarkers of metabolic health and gut functionality. Design: 60 Danish adults at risk of developing metabolic syndrome were included in a randomised cross-over trial with two 8-week dietary intervention periods comprising whole grain diet and refined grain diet, separated by a washout period of ≥6 weeks. The response to the interventions on the gut microbiome composition and insulin sensitivity as well on measures of glucose and lipid metabolism, gut functionality, inflammatory markers, anthropometry and urine metabolomics were assessed. Results: 50 participants completed both periods with a whole grain intake of 179±50 g/day and 13±10 g/day in the whole grain and refined grain period, respectively. Compliance was confirmed by a difference in plasma alkylresorcinols (p<0.0001). Compared with refined grain, whole grain did not significantly alter glucose homeostasis and did not induce major changes in the faecal microbiome. Also, breath hydrogen levels, plasma short-chain fatty acids, intestinal integrity and intestinal transit time were not affected. The whole grain diet did, however, compared with the refined grain diet, decrease body weight (p<0.0001), serum inflammatory markers, interleukin (IL)-6 (p=0.009) and C-reactive protein (p=0.003). The reduction in body weight was consistent with a reduction in energy intake, and IL-6 reduction was associated with the amount of whole grain consumed, in particular with intake of rye. Conclusion: Compared with refined grain diet, whole grain diet did not alter insulin sensitivity and gut microbiome but reduced body weight and systemic low-grade inflammation. Trial Registration Number: NCT 01731366; Results.","author_count":43,"contributors":["Roager, Henrik Munch","Vogt, Josef Korbinian","Kristensen, Mette Bredal","Hansen, Lea Benedicte Skov","Ibrügger, Sabine","Mærkedahl, Rasmus Baadsgaard","Bahl, Martin Iain","Lind, Mads Vendelbo","Nielsen, Rikke Linnemann","Frøkiær, Hanne","Gøbel, Rikke Juul","Landberg, Rikard","Ross, Alastair B.","Brix, Susanne","Holck, Jesper","Meyer, Anne S.","Sparholt, Morten H.","Christensen, Anders Fogh","Carvalho, Vera","Hartmann, Bolette","Holst, Jens Juul","Rumessen, Jüri Johannes","Linneberg, Allan","Sicheritz-Pontén, Thomas","Dalgaard, Marlene D.","Blennow, Andreas","Frandsen, Henrik Lauritz","Villas-Bôas, Silas","Kristiansen, Karsten","Vestergaard, Henrik","Hansen, Torben","Ekstrøm, Claus Thorn","Ritz, Christian","Nielsen, Henrik Bjørn","Pedersen, Oluf","Gupta, Ramneek","Lauritzen, Lotte","Licht, Tine Rask"],"cuid":["S03-099.29-032620"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"corresponding_author":["Gupta, Ramneek"],"country":["New Zealand","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Chalmers University of Technology","The University of Auckland"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Blood Glucose metabolism","Colonic microflora","Cross Over Studies","Denmark","Diet","Energy Intake","Feces microbiology","Female","Gastrointestinal Microbiome","Humans","Immune response","Inflammation","Inflammation blood","Insulin Resistance","Interleukin 6 blood","Lipids blood","Male","Metabolomics","Middle Aged","Obesity","Weight loss","Whole Grains"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"OT":["Steno Diabetes Centre, Gentofte, Denmark."],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"whole grain rich diet reduces body weight and systemic low grade inflammation without inducing major changes of the gut microbiome a randomised cross over trial","src":["dtu","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U