{"abstract":"In this paper we shall discuss how the iPad becomes an actor in socio-material contexts of schooling and how it becomes significant for learning as an artifact with specific affordances within these contexts. We argue that the implementation of the iPad may expose the diversity of artifacts that are already present as actors in learning contexts. As one among many artifacts used in orchestrating learning, the iPad acts in shifting material cultures of schooling where for instance pens, paper, books or interactive whiteboards are used in combination with the tablet. At other times the iPad itself acts as a book that mediates learning in specific ways, for instance by involving the reader through interaction or by new forms of visual representation. In its shifting representational forms, modalities and relationships the iPad provides specific affordances for learning and contributes to the transformation of e.g. textual and visual cultures in areas such as reading and science education. This indicates the need for discussing how curricula knowledge and skills can be understood in the socio-material contexts of learning where iPads are involved. The paper builds on data from two research projects that investigated the iPad as a learning device in the context of primary and lower secondary schooling in Denmark. In these projects learning with the iPad focused on 1) the role of the iPad in inclusive learning environments 2) children’s visual cultures and the development of the iBook iTAVS. Based on these projects the paper will give examples of how empirical observation and analysis can contribute to understanding learning cultures in which iPads are embedded as part of the learning.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Buhl, Mie","Meyer, Bente Tobiesen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-109121"],"event":{"acronym":"ECEL","end":"2014-10-31","place":"AAU København, dk","start":"2014-10-30","title":"ECEL2014","year":"2014"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Socio material cultures of learning","Visual cultures in schooling","learning with iPads"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"one among many the ipad in shifting material cultures of learning","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.academic-bookshop.com/ourshop/prod_3579861-ECEL-2014-13th-European-Conference-on-eLearning-ECEL-2014-Copenhagen-Denmark-PRINT-version.html","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-109121","identifier":[{"id":"WOS:000351434400010","type":"wos"}],"ids":["aau-cb9631c1-27b3-4fc7-9349-58af5becca05"],"keyword":["learning with iPads","socio-material cultures of learning,","visual cultures in schooling"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-4-4-06BDF62BD02844AB5A82A28EEDBFFA13"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.academic-bookshop.com/ourshop/prod_3579861-ECEL-2014-13th-European-Conference-on-eLearning-ECEL-2014-Copenhagen-Denmark-PRINT-version.html","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities, Department of Learning and Philosophy, ILD - Forskningslab i IT og Læringsdesign (Learning)","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology, Kommunikation - IT og Læringsdesign"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mie","key":"buhlpau","last":"Buhl","locid":"d50a1ab2-4c49-4e72-9a34-213eb182bc69","name":"Buhl, Mie","orcid":"0000-0002-2120-4446","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Bente Tobiesen","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","locid":"e0df927b-87d8-4aa1-9287-4a2b5223ae09","name":"Meyer, Bente Tobiesen","orcid":"0000-0003-1198-2196","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Rikke","key":"ørngreenpedh","last":"Ørngreen","name":"Ørngreen, Rikke","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Karin","key":"levinsenpedh","last":"Levinsen","name":"Levinsen, Karin","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9781910309698"],"isbn":["9781910309674"],"pages":"172-179","publisher":"Academic Conferences International (ACI)","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 13th European Conference on E-learning ECEL 2014"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84921653570"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-cb9631c1-27b3-4fc7-9349-58af5becca05"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"One among many : the iPad in shifting material cultures of learning","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-109121","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Abstract Content: The iPad is a relatively new technology in education that promises to provide teachers and learners with new potentials for organizing learning, creating and accessing learning content. Research shows that the iPad can be understood both as a device that provides specific affordances for learning and as an actor in socio-material processes of learning i.e. as one actor among many in education (Burden et al 2012, Melhuish & Falloon 2010, Meyer 2013) . The question is therefore whether and how the iPad in itself can provide the innovation in learning that is expected from the integration of a mobile learning device into education. In this paper we shall discuss how the iPad becomes an actor in socio-material contexts of schooling and how it becomes significant for learning as an artifact with specific affordances within these contexts. We argue that the implementation of the iPad may expose the diversity of artifacts that are already present as actors in learning contexts. As one among many artefacts used in orchestrating learning, the iPad acts in shifting material cultures of schooling where for instance pens, paper, books or interactive whiteboards are used in combination with the tablet. At other times the iPad itself acts as a book that mediates learning in specific ways, for instance by involving the reader through interaction or by new forms of visual representation. In its shifting representational forms, modalities and relationships the iPad provides specific affordances for learning and contributes to the transformation of e.g. textual and visual cultures in areas such as reading and science education. This indicates the need for discussing how curricula knowledge and skills can be understood in the socio-material contexts of learning where iPads are involved. The paper builds on data from two research projects that investigated the iPad as a learning device in the context of primary and lower secondary schooling in Denmark. In these projects learning with the iPad focused on 1) the role of the iPad in inclusive learning environments 2) children’s visual cultures and the development of the iBook ITAVS. Based on these projects the paper will give examples of how empirical observation and analysis can contribute to understanding learning cultures in which iPads are embedded as part of the learning. Theoretically, we are inspired by Actor-Network Theory (Latour 2005, Fenwick and Edwards 2012, Sørensen 2009) semiotics (Kress 2010) and visual culture studies (Mirzoeff 2009, Pauwels 2006). These theoretic frameworks can, we propose, contribute to our understanding of how iPads as one artifact among many - constitute change in socio-material cultures of schooling.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Buhl, Mie","Meyer, Bente Tobiesen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-112505"],"event":{"acronym":"ECEL","end":"2014-10-31","place":"Copenhagen, dk","start":"2014-10-30","title":"13th European Conference on e-Learning","year":"2014"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Socio material cultures of learning","learning with iPads"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"one among many the ipad in shifting material cultures of learning","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://academic-conferences.org/pdfs/ECEL/ECEL2014-Abstract-booklet.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-112505","ids":["aau-d70673b3-e9bb-4b9f-ab44-b91ad77a6ea9"],"keyword":["learning with iPads,","socio-material cultures of learning","socio-material cultures visual cultures in schooling"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-4-4-06BDF62BD02844AB5A82A28EEDBFFA13"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://academic-conferences.org/pdfs/ECEL/ECEL2014-Abstract-booklet.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities, Department of Learning and Philosophy, ILD - Forskningslab i IT og Læringsdesign (Learning)","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology, Kommunikation - IT og Læringsdesign"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mie","key":"buhlpau","last":"Buhl","locid":"d50a1ab2-4c49-4e72-9a34-213eb182bc69","name":"Buhl, Mie","orcid":"0000-0002-2120-4446","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Bente Tobiesen","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","locid":"e0df927b-87d8-4aa1-9287-4a2b5223ae09","name":"Meyer, Bente Tobiesen","orcid":"0000-0003-1198-2196","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Rikke","key":"ørngreenpedh","last":"Ørngreen","name":"Ørngreen, Rikke","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Karin","key":"levinsenpedh","last":"Levinsen","name":"Levinsen, Karin","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["978‐1‐910309‐69‐8"],"isbn":["978‐1‐910309‐67‐4"],"pages":"9","publisher":"Academic Conferences International (ACI)","status":"p","title":"Abstracts of Papers Presented at the 13th European Conference on e‐Learning ECEL‐2014"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84921653570"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-d70673b3-e9bb-4b9f-ab44-b91ad77a6ea9"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"One among many : the iPad in shifting material cultures of learning","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-112505","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"In this paper we shall discuss how the iPad becomes an actor in socio-material contexts of schooling and how it becomes significant for learning as an artifact with specific affordances within these contexts. We argue that the implementation of the iPad may expose the diversity of artifacts that are already present as actors in learning contexts. As one among many artefacts used in orchestrating learning, the iPad acts in shifting material cultures of schooling where for instance pens, paper, books or interactive whiteboards are used in combination with the tablet. At other times the iPad itself acts as a book that mediates learning in specific ways, for instance by involving the reader through interaction or by new forms of visual representation. In its shifting representational forms, modalities and relationships the iPad provides specific affordances for learning and contributes to the transformation of e.g. textual and visual cultures in areas such as reading and science education. This indicates the need for discussing how curricula knowledge and skills can be understood in the socio-material contexts of learning where iPads are involved. The paper builds on data from two research projects that investigated the iPad as a learning device in the context of primary and lower secondary schooling in Denmark. In these projects learning with the iPad focused on 1) the role of the iPad in inclusive learning environments 2) children's visual cultures and the development of the iBook iTAVS. Based on these projects the paper will give examples of how empirical observation and analysis can contribute to understanding learning cultures in which iPads are embedded as part of the learning.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Buhl, Mie","Meyer, Bente Tobiesen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-114861"],"event":{"end":"2014-10-31","place":"Copenhagen, dk","start":"2014-10-30","title":"13th European Conference on e-Learning, ECEL 2014","year":"2014"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Socio material cultures of learning","Visual cultures in schooling","learning with iPads"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"one among many the ipad in shifting material cultures of learning","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://www.scopus.com/inward/record.url?scp=84921653570&partnerID=8YFLogxK","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-114861","identifier":[{"id":"84921653570","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-dee032af-eb5c-4778-8683-e59b019bc87a"],"keyword":["Learning with ipads","Socio-material cultures of learning","Visual cultures in schooling"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-4-4-06BDF62BD02844AB5A82A28EEDBFFA13"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities, Department of Learning and Philosophy, ILD - Forskningslab i IT og Læringsdesign (Learning)","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology, Kommunikation - IT og Læringsdesign"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mie","key":"buhlpau","last":"Buhl","locid":"d50a1ab2-4c49-4e72-9a34-213eb182bc69","name":"Buhl, Mie","orcid":"0000-0002-2120-4446","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bente","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","locid":"e0df927b-87d8-4aa1-9287-4a2b5223ae09","name":"Meyer, Bente Tobiesen","name_org":"Meyer, Bente","orcid":"0000-0003-1198-2196","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Karin Tweddell","key":"levinsenpedh","last":"Levinsen","name":"Levinsen, Karin Tweddell","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Rikke","key":"orngreenpedh","last":"Orngreen","name":"Orngreen, Rikke","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9781910309674"],"pages":"73-80","publisher":"Academic Conferences International (ACI)","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 13th European Conference on e-Learning, ECEL-2014","volume":"2014-January"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84921653570"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-dee032af-eb5c-4778-8683-e59b019bc87a"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"One among many : The iPad in shifting material cultures of learning","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-114861","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Bringing the study of bacterial adhesion down to a single-cell level is critical for understanding the molecular mechanisms involved in initial bacterial attachment. We have developed a simple and versatile method for making single-cell bacterial probes to study the adhesion of single bacterial cells by atomic force microscopy (AFM). A single-cell probe was made by picking up a bacterial cell from a glass surface using a tipless AFM cantilever coated with a commercial cell adhesive Cell-Tak. The method was applied to four different bacterial strains, and single-cell adhesion was measured on three surfaces (fresh glass, hydrophilic glass, and mica). Attachment to the cantilever was stable during the AFM force measurements that were conducted for 2 h, and viability was confirmed by Live/Dead fluorescence staining at the end of each experiment. The adhesion force and final rupture length were dependent on bacterial strains, surfaces properties, and contact time. The single-cell probe offers control of cell immobilization and thus holds advantages over the commonly used multicell probes with which random immobilization is obtained by submerging the cantilever in a bacterial suspension. The reported method provides a general platform for investigating single-cell interactions of bacteria with different surfaces and other cells by AFM force spectroscopy, thus improving our understanding of the mechanisms of bacterial attachment.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Zeng, Guanghong","Müller, Torsten","Meyer, Rikke Louise"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-195397"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"single cell force spectroscopy of bacteria enabled by naturally derived proteins","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/la404673q","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-195397","identifier":[{"id":"24654836","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84898872513","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-73c1ee06-66ae-4857-b063-152e6686d145"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7167539AC6B1E107A38F6CAB91BEC382"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/la404673q","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center - INANO-Bioscience, iNANO-huset"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Guanghong","key":"zengpau","last":"Zeng","locid":"41d64164-8293-4f51-b1b8-96ac2ccc6db5","name":"Zeng, Guanghong","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Torsten","key":"müllerpau","last":"Müller","name":"Müller, Torsten","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rikke L","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","locid":"f1caa4f6-bc6e-459a-a976-8843c7ea7ff3","name":"Meyer, Rikke Louise","name_org":"Meyer, Rikke L","orcid":"0000-0002-6485-5134","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["24654836"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1021/la404673q","issn":["07437463","15205827"],"issn_org":["07437463"],"issue":"14","journal":"Langmuir","pages":"4019-25","publisher":"American Chemical Society","status":"p","title":"Langmuir","volume":"30"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84898872513"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-73c1ee06-66ae-4857-b063-152e6686d145"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Single-cell force spectroscopy of bacteria enabled by naturally derived proteins","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-195397","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"author_count":3,"contributors":["Okshevsky, Mira Ursula","Zeng, Guanghong","Meyer, Rikke Louise"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-214247"],"event":{"end":"2014-11-10","place":"Copenhagen, dk","start":"2014-11-10","title":"Congress of the Danish Microbiological Society","year":"2014"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a sticky situation the differential role of edna proteins and polysaccharides in initial adhesion of i staphylococcus spp i","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dco","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-214247","ids":["au-94ff9aa6-4612-48d9-ba94-b7c54368fd67"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-9E594BDC98AE56AC27CECB31D2CBE177"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center - INANO-Bioscience, iNANO-huset"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mira Ursula","key":"okshevskypau","last":"Okshevsky","locid":"70b6699e-a3fc-49e2-9052-08c02dfa5bae","name":"Okshevsky, Mira Ursula","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Guanghong","key":"zengpau","last":"Zeng","locid":"41d64164-8293-4f51-b1b8-96ac2ccc6db5","name":"Zeng, Guanghong","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rikke Louise","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","locid":"f1caa4f6-bc6e-459a-a976-8843c7ea7ff3","name":"Meyer, Rikke Louise","orcid":"0000-0002-6485-5134","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-94ff9aa6-4612-48d9-ba94-b7c54368fd67"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"A sticky situation: the differential role of eDNA, proteins and polysaccharides in initial adhesion of <i>Staphylococcus spp.</i>","type":"Conference Poster","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-214247","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Bacteria initiate attachment to the surfaces with the aid of different extracellular polymers. To quantitatively study how these polymers mediate bacterial adhesion and possibly their interactions, it is essential to go down to single cell level, with in mind that cell-to-cell variation should be considered. We have therefore developed a simple and versatile method to make single-cell bacterial probes for measuring single cell adhesion with atomic force microscopy (AFM).[1] A single-cell probe was readily made by picking up a bacterial cell from a glass surface using a tipless AFM cantilever coated with a commercial cell adhesive CellTakTM. The method was applied to four different bacterial strains, and single-cell adhesion was measured on three surfaces (fresh glass, hydrophilic glass, mica). Attachment to the cantilever was stable during the 2 h of AFM force measurements, and viability was confirmed by Live/Dead fluorescence staining at the end of each experiment. The adhesion force and final rupture length were dependent on bacterial strains, surfaces properties, and time of contact. The single-cell probe offers control of the cell immobilization, thus holds advantages over the commonly used multi-cell probes where random immobilization is obtained by submerging the cantilever in a bacterial suspension. The reported method provides a general platform for investigating single cell interactions of bacteria with different surfaces and other cells by AFM force spectroscopy, thus improving our understanding of the mechanisms of bacterial attachment. An alternative way to study the adhesion of single bacterial cells is to measure the adhesion between immobilized bacterial cells and coated AFM cantilevers. This strategy was used to investigate the adhesive properties of novel high density poly(ethylene glycol) (PEG) coatings on titanium. We investigate the ability of a high density poly(L-lysine)-graft-poly(ethylene glycol) (PLL-g-PEG) coating to resist bacterial adhesion and biofilm formation from three clinically relevant bacteria: Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Staphylococcus aureus and Staphylococcus epidermis. The high density PLL-g-PEG coatings were about eight times as thick as the conventional PLL-g-PEG coatings. Adhesion forces toward high density PLL-g-PEG coatings were low (P. aeruginosa) or close to zero (S. aureus and S. epidermidis) compared to bare titanium surface. However, no decrease in adhesion force was observed for S. epidermidis toward conventional PLL-g-PEG coatings, whereas significantly lower adhesion forces were observed for S. aureus and P. aeruginosa. The adhesion force patterns were reflected by the colonization of bacteria after 48 h incubation of the coatings in bacterial cultures. The high density PLL-g-PEG coatings completely resisted bacterial colonization, whereas conventional coatings couldn’t resist colonization by S. epidermidis. The unique ability of S. epidermidis to colonize conventional PLL-g-PEG coatings was investigated by looking into the composition of S. epidermidis biofilm. The strain-dependent susceptibility to bacterial colonization on conventional PLL-g-PEG illustrates how bacterial diversity challenges development of “universal” antifouling coatings, and AFM single-cell force spectroscopy was proven to be a powerful tool to provide insights into the molecular mechanisms of adhesion by different bacterial strains.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Zeng, Guanghong","Ogaki, Ryosuke","Regina, Viduthalai R.","Müller, Torsten","Meyer, Rikke Louise"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-254942"],"event":{"end":"2014-09-06","place":"Toronto, ca","start":"2014-09-02","title":"SPM 2014","year":"2014"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"probing living bacterial adhesion by single cell force spectroscopy using atomic force microscopy","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-254942","ids":["au-dc0f62c8-27e9-4ec8-9906-bf55658277b1"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-0EDE441DB25D0F1A5CBE0FEDFB3A42DF"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center - INANO-Bioscience, iNANO-huset"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Guanghong","key":"zengpau","last":"Zeng","locid":"41d64164-8293-4f51-b1b8-96ac2ccc6db5","name":"Zeng, Guanghong","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Ryosuke","key":"ogakipau","last":"Ogaki","locid":"3ee7810d-e08c-49e2-988e-20c5f9b98a85","name":"Ogaki, Ryosuke","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Viduthalai R.","key":"reginapau","last":"Regina","locid":"fa8a7634-7c35-4495-9bf4-78035ae9c80e","name":"Regina, Viduthalai R.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Torsten","key":"müllerpau","last":"Müller","name":"Müller, Torsten","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rikke Louise","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","locid":"f1caa4f6-bc6e-459a-a976-8843c7ea7ff3","name":"Meyer, Rikke Louise","orcid":"0000-0002-6485-5134","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-dc0f62c8-27e9-4ec8-9906-bf55658277b1"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Probing living bacterial adhesion by single cell force spectroscopy using atomic force microscopy","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-254942","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Extracellular DNA (eDNA) is an important structural component of biofilms formed by many bacteria, but few reports have focused on its role in initial cell adhesion. The aim of this study was to investigate the role of eDNA in bacterial adhesion to abiotic surfaces, and determine to which extent eDNA-mediated adhesion depends on the physicochemical properties of the surface and surrounding liquid. We investigated eDNA alteration of cell surface hydrophobicity and zeta potential, and subsequently quantified the effect of eDNA on the adhesion of Staphylococcus xylosus to glass surfaces functionalised with different chemistries resulting in variable hydrophobicity and charge. Cell adhesion experiments were carried out at three different ionic strengths. Removal of eDNA from S. xylosus cells by DNase treatment did not alter the zeta potential, but rendered the cells more hydrophilic. DNase treatment impaired adhesion of cells to glass surfaces, but the adhesive properties of S. xylosus were regained within 30 minutes if DNase was not continuously present, implying a continuous release of eDNA in the culture. Removal of eDNA lowered the adhesion of S. xylosus to all surfaces chemistries tested, but not at all ionic strengths. No effect was seen on glass surfaces and carboxyl-functionalised surfaces at high ionic strength, and a reverse effect occurred on amine-functionalised surfaces at low ionic strength. However, eDNA promoted adhesion of cells to hydrophobic surfaces irrespective of the ionic strength. The adhesive properties of eDNA in mediating initial adhesion of S. xylosus is thus highly versatile, but also dependent on the physicochemical properties of the surface and ionic strength of the surrounding medium.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Regina, Viduthalai R.","Lokanathan, Arcot R.","Modrzynski, Jakub","Sutherland, Duncan S","Meyer, Rikke Louise"],"cuid":["S02-087.23-023172"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-UND"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-UND"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"surface physicochemistry and ionic strength affects edna s role in bacterial adhesion to abiotic surfaces","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/130558690/journal.pone.0105033.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-087.23-023172","identifier":[{"id":"25122477","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84905986480","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC4133339","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-90026827-7e3e-4e28-b399-1d24d674b410","ku-fa22bff8-e38e-4831-bee4-856bf4007fb1"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-0DCA1D3B85FCFD4D1C9D2665AE5A28E5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0105033","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/130558690/journal.pone.0105033.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center - INANO-Bioscience, iNANO-huset"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"unclear universities","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-UND","l3nl":"Undetermined","l3nn":"Unclear Universities","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Unclear Universities","namevar":["Interdisciplinær Nanoscience Forskerskole"],"nora_code":"DI-U-UND","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Plant and Environmental Sciences, Microbial Ecology and Biotechnology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Viduthalai R.","key":"reginapau","last":"Regina","locid":"fa8a7634-7c35-4495-9bf4-78035ae9c80e","name":"Regina, Viduthalai R.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Arcot R","key":"lokanathanpau","last":"Lokanathan","locid":"56fe202c-b628-4857-93b8-3cc3a66f554b","name":"Lokanathan, Arcot R.","name_org":"Lokanathan, Arcot R","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Jakub","key":"modrzynskipau","last":"Modrzynski","locid":"e195895c-9829-42b5-bbcc-655fa436b21f","name":"Modrzynski, Jakub","orcid":"0000-0001-6297-5741","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Duncan S","key":"sutherlandpau","last":"Sutherland","locid":"5c5c563f-ca05-4219-b60d-8a3d6303ff2f","name":"Sutherland, Duncan S","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rikke L","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","locid":"f1caa4f6-bc6e-459a-a976-8843c7ea7ff3","name":"Meyer, Rikke Louise","name_org":"Meyer, Rikke L","orcid":"0000-0002-6485-5134","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["25122477"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0105033","issn":["19326203"],"issue":"8","journal":"PLOS ONE","paperid":"e105033","publisher":"Public Library of Science","status":"p","title":"PLOS ONE","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84905986480"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Undetermined","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-90026827-7e3e-4e28-b399-1d24d674b410","ku-fa22bff8-e38e-4831-bee4-856bf4007fb1"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Surface Physicochemistry and Ionic Strength Affects eDNA's Role in Bacterial Adhesion to Abiotic Surfaces","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-087.23-023172","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
